174:
In order to be eligible for a consumption on the premises license, a supper club must:
(1) Have a seating capacity, at tables, of at least 100 persons;
(2) Have adequate and sanitary full-service kitchen facilities and staff to prepare, cook, and serve suitable food for its patrons, except that this requirement shall not apply to a supper club which is located in a hotel having separate kitchen facilities of which the supper club makes use;
(3) Provide a band or other professional entertainment a minimum of 20 days in each calendar month, each and every calendar month of the calendar year, with the exception of holidays, vacations, and periods of redecorating;
(4) Be arranged and maintained such that all booths and tables are open and unobstructed to the view of other persons in the facility;
(5) Serve at least one meal per day at least five days per week, with the exception of holidays, vacations, and periods of redecoration; and
(6) Have at least 51 percent of its total sales the sale of food and non-alcoholic beverages consumed on the premises, exclusive of sales from vending machines; and for this purpose, if a supper club makes a minimum charge or cover charge, the amount so charged shall not be counted in computing total sales and shall not be counted as a food or beverage sale.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

178:
(a) To be eligible for a consumption on the premises license, a golf club must:
(1) Be a corporation or association organized and existing under the laws of the State of Georgia and actively in operation within the city prior to submitting an application for a license hereunder;
(2) Be a recognized member of the Georgia State Golf Association;
(3) Operate at least one 18-hole golf course within its grounds of least a par 70 as determined in accordance with the standards of the Georgia State Golf Association or the United States Golf Association;
(4) Operate a restaurant:
a. Within an enclosed structure with a full-service kitchen used exclusively for the purpose of preparing and serving meals by staff trained to prepare, cook, and serve meals and other suitable food for patrons;
b. With a seating capacity of at least 60 patrons, with "seating capacity" meaning that no more than 25 percent of such seating shall be at a common table or counter area or shall be other than individual tables or booths designed for seating of at least two individuals; and
c. That prepares and serves meals at least six days a week, with the exception of weeks including holidays, vacation and periods of renovation;
(5) Have a golf club membership of at least 100 members at the time an application for a license is submitted who have paid a membership fee for family or individual membership; and
(6) Employ a full-time management staff responsible for planning and directing the social activities of the golf club, including the management of the areas where food and drink are sold and dispensed.
(b) Provided that the foregoing requirements are satisfied and that all other applicable requirements of this section are satisfied, alcoholic beverages dispensed by the golf club to patrons from any location on the premises may be consumed by the patrons anywhere on the premises of the golf club.
(c) The notice required by section 6-7 shall be posted prominently at all locations where alcoholic beverages are dispensed and at the exit of each structure from which alcoholic beverages are dispensed.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

179:
(a) To be eligible for a consumption on the premises license, an indoor recreation establishment must:
(1) Have as its primary purpose making available an indoor physical, non-sedentary, recreational activity that requires use of specialized equipment to be made available for a fee and that occurs within a defined area of the establishment's premises that is physically segregated and separate from the remainder of the establishment's premises;
(2) Provide that the defined area for the recreational activity consist of a rink, court(s), or other recreational area that is suitable and customary for the recreational activity to be undertaken;
(3) Prohibit the use or presence of alcohol within the defined area required by this section;
(4) Must make food available for consumption by patrons and guests of the establishment;
(5) Contain an adequate and sanitary kitchen facility approved by the county health department; employ staff to prepare, cook, and serve suitable food for guests; and serve at least one meal per day at least five days per week with the exception of holidays, vacations, periods of redecoration or remodeling, and other occasions where the establishment is temporarily closed; and
(6) Must derive 70 percent of its total food and alcoholic beverage sales from the sale of food.
(b) Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following are excluded from the definition of an indoor recreational facility and are ineligible for a consumption on the premises license under this section:
(1) Health clubs and spas;
(2) Health and fitness centers and similar exercise facilities;
(3) Martial arts studios and other similar establishments;
(4) Facilities used for the purpose of providing personal training services; and
(5) Gymnasiums.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

181:
All lounge and restaurant areas, including all tables, booths, and other areas where customers are served and including all passageways for customers, shall be sufficiently well illuminated so that they may be viewed by those inside the premises. The sale or dispensing of alcoholic beverages in any back room or side room that is not open to the general public is prohibited, except that this prohibition shall not apply with respect to:
(1) Private parties which have been scheduled in advance;
(2) Sales to hotel guests in their hotel rooms;
(3) Private clubs; or
(4) Special events facilities.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

183:
Bands or orchestras and patron dancing shall be permitted at facilities licensed for consumption on the premises sales only where:
(1) Adequate space exists;
(2) All fire and safety regulations are met;
(3) Prior approval of the chief of police and the county fire marshal has been obtained.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

189:
(a) Every licensee shall keep and preserve records of all alcoholic beverages purchased and sold or otherwise dispensed by the licensee. All consumption on the premises licensees shall keep and preserve records of all food and nonalcoholic beverages purchased and sold or otherwise dispensed by them. Such records, more specifically described in subsection (c) of this section, shall at all times be open for inspection by the city clerk or his designee. These records shall be maintained for a period of at least three years unless the city manager determines that no such records exist and that it is not financially practical based on the net income of the licensee to require the keeping of such records.
(b) If the city manager deems it advisable to conduct an audit of the records of a licensee, he shall notify the licensee of the date, time, and place of the audit. The city manager may designate the city's internal auditor or other person to perform the audit, and the licensee shall cooperate with the audit or be subject to having his license suspended or revoked.
(c) At the request of the city manager or his designee, the licensee shall make available the following records required to be kept for at least three years:
(1) Monthly income or operating statements;
(2) Daily sales receipts showing liquor, beer, wine, and food sales separately (this requirement does not apply to package beer and wine licensees);
(3) Daily cash register receipts such as Z tapes or guest tickets;
(4) Monthly state sales and use tax reports; and
(5) Federal Income Tax returns with all Form 1099s.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

197:
(a) A licensee shall file with the city manager or police chief a written, sworn report of any of the following changes:
(1) Any change in any legal relationship between any parties named in the application for the license;
(2) Any change in the payment of rent for leased premises or any change in the ownership of the licensed premises;
(3) Any loss or damage to goods or premises of the licensee which results in a claim against an insurance policy;
(4) Any change for any purpose in division of net or gross sales of the licensed business; and
(5) Any change in any material facts contained in the application for the license.
(b) Such report shall be filed within five days after the date the change occurs.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

205:
(a) No consumption on the premises licensee shall engage in any of the following practices:
(1) The sale of alcoholic beverages during any special period of the day at prices lower than those customarily charged during the remainder of the day;
(2) The giving away of any alcoholic beverages in conjunction with the sale of any other alcoholic beverages;
(3) The sale of two or more alcoholic beverages for a single price or the sale of all the alcoholic beverages a customer can or desires to drink; provided, however, nothing herein shall prohibit a brewpub from offering a sampler of no more than four types of malt beverages in containers not exceeding four ounces each;
(4) The sale or serving of two or more alcoholic beverages at substantially the same price customarily charged for one alcoholic beverage;
(5) Requiring or encouraging the purchase of a second or subsequent alcoholic beverage at the same time another alcoholic beverage is purchased or before the first such beverage has been consumed;
(6) Selling distilled spirits in containers or glasses containing more than one and one-half ounces of distilled spirits;
(7) Sponsoring, conducting, or allowing contests or allowing contests or promotions which have as their primary purpose the increasing of the consumption of alcoholic beverages on the premises.
(b) This section shall not apply with respect to private functions not open to the public with respect to which the licensee has agreed to the use of the licensee's establishment by a person, firm, or organization for a set period of time for a valuable consideration.
(Ord. No. 2017-06, § 1(Exh. A), 3-7-2017)

217:
Unless the context indicates otherwise, the meaning of certain terms used in this chapter is as follows:
City means the City of Hiram, Georgia.
Governing authority means the mayor and council of the City of Hiram.
Producer means any person, group of persons, organization, association, club, or other entity responsible for planning, producing, or conducting a special event.
Special event means any temporary event, activity, or gathering that is made available for public attendance or participation that will occur on public property and/or on private property where the number of persons participating in the event, activity, or gathering on the property will exceed 20 or which will require or involve one or more of the following activities: closing (full or partial) of a public street; blocking or obstructing public property; the provision of merchandise, food, or beverages (whether by sale or otherwise) where not otherwise allowed; the erection, use, or installation of a tent, canopy, stage, trailer, grandstand, bleachers, or any other structure; use of any kind of pyrotechnics or sound amplification devices; or placement and use of portable toilets (except where designated for use as part of ongoing construction on the property). Examples of "special events" include, but are not limited to, fairs; festivals; "fun runs," walks, and other kinds of races, including bicycle races; parades; rallies; concerts; tours; and block parties.
(1) For purposes of this chapter, a special event does not include:
a. Gatherings or activities occurring on private property that are not open for public attendance or participation and which make no use of city streets, alleys, or rights-of-way except as otherwise permitted for lawful access or lawful parking;
b. Gatherings or activities occurring on city park property that are intended to be private events and do not significantly vary from the customary use of the property (e.g., family cookouts, birthday parties);
c. Regularly scheduled or occurring events on city park property by persons, organizations, or other entities with ongoing agreements with the city for the use of city park property;
d. Garage sales, estate sales, lawn sales, rummage sales, or any similar casual, sporadic sale of goods and merchandise by the owner of the property on which the event or activity is conducted;
e. The display of goods, merchandise, or services for sale on a public sidewalk or other public property immediately adjacent to the store or space by an ongoing, lawfully licensed business that occupies the immediately adjacent store or space, but excluding those subject to the commercial soliciting ordinance found at chapter 12, article IX of the city's Code of Ordinances, so long as such displays do not impede the normal flow of pedestrian or vehicular traffic and so long as other applicable laws and ordinances that may pertain to such activity are satisfied;
f. Any procession conducted under the supervision and direction of a funeral director in conjunction with a funeral; and
Vendor means any person, group of persons, organization, association, club, or other entity that engages in the sale to the public of any food or food products, goods, services, or merchandise of whatever nature from any location, either mobile or stationary, on a temporary basis on any public street, sidewalk, right-of-way, or private property as an authorized participant in a special event. Vendors participating as part of a special event for which a lawful permit has been issued are excused from complying with the city's commercial soliciting ordinance for the duration of the vendors' participation in the special event.
(Ord. No. 2014-02, § l(Exh. A), 5-6-2014; Ord. No. 2024-09, § 1 (Exh. A), 7-2-2024)

224:
(a) Upon submission of a complete application and all information and materials required by this chapter, payment of any required fee, receipt of all information from government departments per section 7-8 above, the city manager shall, within three business days, act on the request for a special event permit unless additional time is provided by agreement with the producer. If additional time is necessary due to the absence of the city manager and the producer does not agree to additional time to permit the city manager's review of the application and materials, the application may be denied for lack of appropriate review.
(b) A permit shall be issued where the city manager finds that:
(1) The application is complete and all materials and information required in support of the application has been provided and the information provided is reasonably determined to be accurate;
(2) The event will not unduly inconvenience the public in using public areas normally open for general public use or the city in conducting governmental operations;
(3) Adequate provision is or will be made for responding to medical and other public safety incidents and emergencies occurring during the special event;
(4) Adequate provision is or will be made for parking, vehicular/pedestrian traffic, crowd and traffic control, and security along with plans for restroom facilities and the collection and removal of rubbish, garbage, and any other waste byproducts generated by the special event;
(5) The producer has paid all fees required and otherwise provided, where required, for payment of other costs and expenses otherwise necessitated by or reasonably anticipated for the event; and
(6) Neither any other event is scheduled elsewhere in the city nor any other objectively determined set of conditions exist whereby city resources (including, but not limited to, law enforcement or public works personnel) required for the events and circumstances are so great that deployment would have an immediate and adverse effect upon the public welfare, health, safety, or good order;
(c) Conditions may be imposed with issuance of a permit to reasonably address concerns described in subsection (b) above.
(d) A special event permit may be revoked or denied by the city upon determining that:
(1) False or incomplete information was provided on or in support of the permit application;
(2) The producer has not complied with all terms and conditions of the permit and any other applicable laws, including the requirements of this or any other city ordinance;
(3) The fees, costs, and expenses required have not been paid or acceptable, alternative arrangements have not been made with the city and confirmed in writing by the city for payment of same; or
(4) There exists an emergency or exigent circumstances such as a disaster or public calamity that impacts the public safety, health, welfare, and good order to such an extent that it is reasonably prudent to revoke or deny the permit.
(e) If an application for a permit is denied or issued subject to additional conditions, or if a permit is revoked, the city manager shall notify the producer in writing of the action, the reason(s) for same, and the right to an appeal. If the permit is revoked, the special event may not proceed and, if revoked while ongoing, the special event must cease immediately.
(f) If an application for a permit is denied or issued subject to additional conditions, or if a permit is revoked, the producer may request in writing that the action be reviewed by the governing authority. Such a request must be submitted to the city clerk within five days of the action complained of and must state with specificity the grounds for the appeal.
(1) The hearing before the governing authority shall be scheduled for a date within 14 days of the filing of the appeal. The governing authority has the discretion, upon determining that exigent circumstances warrant, to schedule and hold an earlier meeting to consider an appeal.
(2) The appeal hearing shall be limited to the grounds stated in the appeal unless, for good cause shown, the governing authority determines to hear other grounds. The city manager and other persons directly affected by the decision at issue may speak and present evidence at the hearing and the parties may be represented by counsel at the appeal hearing.
(3) The governing authority shall issue its decision on the appeal within five days of concluding the hearing.
(g) A decision upholding denial, grant with additional conditions, or revocation of a permit may be appealed by way of a writ of certiorari to the Superior Court of Paulding County.
(Ord. No. 2014-02, § l(Exh. A), 5-6-2014; Ord. No. 2024-09, § 1 (Exh. A), 7-2-2024)

243:
(a) It shall be unlawful for any person by commission or omission to:
(1) Overdrive, overload, overwork, torture, beat, mutilate, kill needlessly, carry or confine in a vehicle in an inhumane manner, or otherwise mistreat any animal;
(2) Fail to provide any animal they own or provide care for with proper food and veterinary care;
(3) Fail to provide any animal they own or provide care for with access to adequate shelter to protect it from all types of weather, 24 hours daily;
(4) Fail to provide any animal they own or provide care for access to good and wholesome fresh water at all times;
(5) Intentionally poison any domestic animal;
(6) Allow or permit any fight between animals, or to allow or permit a fight in or upon any premises in that person's possession or under that person's control;
(7) Allow an animal they own or provide care for to be kept in unsanitary conditions;
(8) Keep or confine any animal in other than a humane manner.
(b) It shall be unlawful to release any animal on any property with the intent to abandon the animal. Each person who does abandon, knowingly or willingly permits such abandonment or aids in the abandonment of any animal, shall be in violation of this chapter.

275:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Business means any person, sole proprietor, partnership, corporation, trade, profession, occupation or other entity and the efforts or activities associated thereby for the purposes of raising revenue or producing income, including, but not limited to, sole proprietors receiving a form IRS 1099 from another business at the same location.
Dominant line means the type of business, within a multiple-line business, from which the greatest amount of income is derived.
Employee means an individual whose work is performed under the direction and supervision of the employer and whose employer withholds FICA, federal income tax, or state income tax from such individual's compensation, or whose employer issues to such individuals, for purposes of documenting compensation, a form I.R.S. W-2 but not a form I.R.S. 1099.
Gross receipts.
(1) The term "gross receipts" means the total revenue of the business or practitioner for the period including, without limitation, the following:
a. Total income without deduction for the cost of goods or expenses incurred;
b. Gain from trading in stocks, bonds, capital assets, or instruments of indebtedness;
c. Proceeds from commissions on the sale of property, goods, or services;
d. Proceeds from fees for services rendered; and
e. Proceeds from rent, interest, royalty, or dividend income.
(2) The term "gross receipts" does not include the following:
a. Sales, use, or excise tax;
b. Sales returns, allowances, and discounts;
c. Interorganizational sales or transfers between or among the units of a parent-subsidiary controlled group of corporations as defined by 26 USC 1563(a)(1), or between or among the units of a brother-sister controlled group of corporations as defined by 26 USC 1563(a)(2);
d. Payments made to a subcontractor or an independent agent for services which contributed to the gross receipts in issue;
e. Governmental and foundation grants, charitable contributions, or the interest income derived from such funds received by a nonprofit organization which employs salaried practitioners otherwise covered by this article, if such funds constitute 80 percent or more of the organization's receipts; and
f. Proceeds from sales of goods or services which are delivered to or received by customers who are outside the state at the time of delivery or receipt.
Location or office means any structure or vehicle where a business, profession, or occupation is conducted, but shall not include a temporary or construction work site which serves a single customer or project or a vehicle used for sales or delivery by a business or practitioner of a profession or occupation which has a location or office. The renter's or lessee's location which is the site of personal property which is rented or leased from another does not constitute a location or office for the personal property's owner, lessor, or the agent of the owner or lessor. The site of real property which is rented or leased to another does not constitute a location or office for the real property's owner, lessor, or the agent of the owner or lessor unless the real property's owner, lessor, or the agent of the owner or lessor, in addition to showing the property to prospective lessees or tenants and performing maintenance or repair of the property, otherwise conducts the business of renting or leasing the real property at such site or otherwise conducts any other business, profession, or occupation at such site.
Occupation tax means a tax levied on persons, partnerships, corporations, or other entities for engaging in an occupation, profession, or business and enacted by the local government as a revenue-raising ordinance or resolution.
Practitioner of profession or occupation means one who by state law requires state licensure regulating such profession or occupation. Practitioners of professions and occupations shall not include a practitioner who is an employee of a business, if the business pays an occupation tax.
Regulatory fees means payments, whether designated as license fees, permit fees, or by another name, which are required by the city as an exercise of its police power and as a part of or an aid to regulation of an occupation, profession, or business. The amount of a regulatory fee shall approximate the reasonable cost of the actual regulatory activity performed by the city. A regulatory fee may not include an administrative fee, registration fee, or fee by any other name in connection with a regulatory fee. Regulatory fees do not include required occupation taxes for businesses and professions located in the city.
(Ord. No. 2015-09, § 1(Exh. A), 11-10-2015)

277:
(a) Any person engaged in any business, trade, profession, or occupation in the city shall submit an occupation tax certificate application with supporting documentation showing compliance with all applicable provisions of state, county, and city laws relating to their business, trade, profession, or occupation.
(b) Any person who performs any business, occupation or profession subject to an occupation tax in the city is required to provide the following information when paying such occupation tax:
(1) The legal name of the business;
(2) Any associated trade names for the business;
(3) The mailing address for the business;
(4) The actual physical address of each location of the business, if it is different than the mailing address;
(5) A detailed description of all business activity(ies);
(6) The North American Industry Classification System code (NAICS) applicable to such business, based on the dominant line of business; and
(7) The sales and use tax identification number assigned to the business by the state department of revenue, if the business is required by law to have such a number.
(8) Federal tax identification number.
(9) Taxable gross receipts as defined under section 12-16 in the form of an affidavit provided by the city.
(10) Any additional information and documents as required by the city.
(11) Each person who is licensed by the secretary of state pursuant to O.C.G.A. tit. 43 shall provide evidence of proper and current state licensure before the city occupation tax certificate may be issued.
(12) Each person who is licensed by the state shall post the state license in a conspicuous place in the licensee's place of business and shall keep the license there at all times while the license remains valid. This requirement to display the license shall not apply to lawyers.
(Ord. No. 2015-09, § 1(Exh. A), 11-10-2015)

289:
(a) An occupation tax certificate may be denied, suspended or revoked upon one or more of the following grounds:
(1) The applicant or certificate holder is guilty of fraud in the operation of the business or occupation he practices or fraud or deceit in being licensed to practice in that area;
(2) The applicant or certificate holder is engaged in the business or occupation under a false or assumed name, or is impersonating another practitioner of a like or different name;
(3) The applicant or certificate holder is addicted to the habitual use of intoxicating liquors, narcotics, or stimulants to such an extent he is unable to perform his duties under the business or occupation;
(4) The applicant or certificate holder is guilty of fraudulent, false, misleading, or deceptive advertising or practices;
(5) The applicant or certificate holder has been convicted of or has pled guilty or nolo contendere to any sexual offense as set out in O.C.G.A. § 16-6-1 et seq., or to any offense involving the lottery, illegal possession or sale of narcotics or alcoholic beverages or possession or receiving of stolen property, for a period of five years immediately prior to the filing of the application. If, after having been granted a certificate, the applicant pleads guilty, is convicted of, or enters a plea of nolo contendere to any of the above offenses, said certificate shall be subject to suspension and/or revocation;
(6) The original application, any supporting document, or renewal thereof, contains materially false information, or the applicant has deliberately sought to falsify information contained therein;
(7) Failure of the business to provide books and records for inspection within 30 days of a demand by the city manager or designee as provided for in section 12-22.
(8) The business or establishment is not authorized to operate within the city, or within the zoning district within which it is located, or is otherwise not in conformity with locational requirements of any of the city's ordinances;
(9) The applicant or licensee has failed to pay any fee to the city or county, has failed to make a return or pay a tax due to the tax commissioner of the county, or any other agency of the city or county government or has otherwise failed to comply with the provisions of this chapter or any other chapter of this Code of Ordinances.
(10) The business or establishment is a threat or nuisance to public health, safety or welfare;
(11) The business or establishment has been found by a court of law to have been operating unlawfully;
(12) Any other violation of this article; or
(13) Violation of another statute, ordinance, rule, or regulation that governs the operation of the business in question.
(b) Within 45 days of the filing of a completed application, the city shall either issue an occupation tax certificate to the applicant or issue a written notice of intent to deny an occupation tax certificate for one or more reasons set forth in subsections (a)(1) through (a)(13) of this section. The notice shall be sent by certified mail, return receipt requested. The written notice shall also notify the applicant of the right to appeal said decision as designated in this section.
(c) Should the city manager or designee find cause for one or more reasons set forth in subsections (a)(1) through (a)(13) of this section for an occupation tax certificate to be suspended or revoked, the city manager or designee shall notify the occupation tax certificate holder by written notice of intent to suspend or revoke said occupation tax certificate. The notice shall be sent by certified mail, return receipt requested. Such notice shall state the grounds for the suspension or revocation of said occupation tax certificate and notify the holder of the right to appeal said decision as designated in this section.
(d) The city manager or designee shall administer and enforce the provisions of this article. Should an aggrieved person or entity desire to appeal a decision under this article, the following procedure shall apply: a notice of appeal must be filed with the city manager or designee within 15 calendar days after receipt of the decision. The notice of appeal shall be in the form of a letter and shall clearly identify all of the objections or exceptions taken to the decision of the city. The notice of appeal shall also contain an address for receipt of future notices. Should the aggrieved person or entity fail to file a notice of appeal within the time allowed, the right to appeal is lost.
(e) Upon receipt of a timely and proper notice of appeal, the city manager or designee shall notify the appellant, in writing, of the date, time and place where a hearing will be held. The notice shall specify the time, place and date, not less than ten days nor more than 30 days after the date the notice is issued on which the mayor and city council, or such hearing officer or board as the mayor and council may designate, shall conduct a hearing on the city manager or designee's written notice of intent to deny, suspend, or revoke the occupation tax certificate. Said hearing shall be conducted in accordance with the administrative hearing procedures as adopted by resolution of the city council.
(f) The mayor and city council, or such hearing officer or board as the mayor and council may designate, shall issue a written decision, including specific reasons for the decision pursuant to this article, to the respondent within five days after the hearing. If the decision is to deny, suspend, or revoke an occupation tax certificate, the decision shall become final unless the applicant or certificate holder files an appeal by petition for writ of certiorari to the county superior court within 30 days of the date of the decision. In case of intent to deny, if the decision concludes that no grounds exist for denial, of the occupation tax certificate, the city shall, within three business days of the issuance of the decision, issue the occupation tax certificate to the applicant.
(g) This section shall not apply to attorneys in the practice of law.
(Ord. No. 2015-09, § 1(Exh. A), 11-10-2015)

338:
(a) Every person who works in a pawnshop is required to have a permit issued by the city. No permit shall be issued unless the person satisfies the requirements in this section or as otherwise required by the article. The permit required by this section expires annually on the date established generally for the business license of the pawnshop, and each person is required to renew their permit by that date. The permit required by this section must be in the possession of the person while performing services at the pawnshop or elsewhere on behalf of the licensee and must be available for inspection. For purposes of this section "person" includes, but is not limited to, employees, the licensee and, if a corporation, its officers, directors, and shareholders and, if a partnership, its partners, as well as the designated responsible person. It is the duty of the licensee to ensure compliance with the provisions of this section.
(1) No licensee shall allow any person to work on the premises or undertake work on behalf of the licensee unless the person has in his or her possession a current, valid city permit, and licensees are required to inspect and verify that each such person has in his or her possession a valid, current pawnshop permit.
(b) The following must be provided in connection with seeking a permit:
(1) A completed and signed application filed with the chief of police or his designee. The application shall, at a minimum, require the applicant's name, residential address, telephone number, email address, social security number, date of birth, and a description of the applicant's prior arrest record that shall only be used for investigative purposes and shall not give rise to a presumption or inference of guilt. The applicant must also provide proof of identify at the time of submitting the application, and only official, government-issued, photo identification is acceptable (e.g., driver's license, passport, military card, or state identification card). The city may from time to time require additional information of applicants;
(2) A SAVE affidavit, a secure and verifiable document and immigration documents (if applicable) indicating the applicant is a U.S. citizen or otherwise qualified alien;
(3) A signed consent form authorizing the chief of police or his designee to conduct a criminal background check on the applicant. The chief of police or his designee shall conduct a complete and exhaustive search relative to any police record of the applicant; and
(4) Payment of an administrative fee in an amount of $25.00.
(c) Persons are ineligible for a permit if:
(1) They have been convicted of or pled guilty or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any crime involving moral turpitude, illegal gambling, any felonies, or any misdemeanor crimes involving theft, deception or fraudulent practices within ten years preceding the date of application. For purposes of this article, a conviction or plea of guilty or nolo contendere entered under the Georgia First Offender Act, O.C.G.A. § 42-8-60 et seq., shall not be considered. Provided, however, that any such offense shall be considered and be deemed disqualifying where the applicant violated any term of probation imposed by the court granting first offender status or committed another crime and the sentencing court entered an adjudication of guilt as to the crime for which the defendant had previously been sentenced as a first offender;
(2) They are subject to outstanding criminal warrants, criminal charges, accusations or indictments for any of the crimes enumerated in this section on which there has been no final disposition or adjudication whereupon any application involving such pending charges shall be held for a final decision until final disposition or adjudication of the charges;
(3) For purposes of working in or on behalf of a pawnshop that deals in firearms, a person must be eligible under then prevailing state law to possess a firearm, and the person must retain eligibility throughout employment in accordance with state law as it may be amended from time to time;
(4) If it is determined that they falsified, concealed, or misrepresented any material fact by any device, trick, or scheme while making application to the City for a permit under this section. No permit shall be deemed effective absent compliance with this provision, and a failure to comply constitutes abandonment of and voids the application and permit sought;
(5) They have previously violated any provision of this article; or
(6) They are a city employee who is directly involved in the licensing, permitting, and regulation of pawnshops.
(d) In the event the applicant satisfies the criteria established in this section and may otherwise be applicable in this article, the chief of police or his designee shall notify the applicant that a permit has been issued. The applicant must make himself or herself available, within one week of notification, to the chief of police or his designee for photographing for the purpose of issuing the permit.
(e) If the permit is denied, the chief of police or his designee shall notify the applicant in writing with a description of the reason(s) for the denial and informing the applicant of the right to appeal the denial to the city manager. Any appeal to the city manager must be presented in writing to the city clerk within ten days of the date of the written denial, and the document filed with the city clerk must provide an explanation by the applicant of the reason for the appeal. Within ten days the city manager shall inform the applicant in writing whether the denial is upheld or reversed. The decision of the city manager is final, subject to review in the Superior Court of Paulding County by way of a petition for a writ of certiorari in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 5-4-1 et seq.
(f) Any permit issued in contravention of this section, article, or any other applicable provision of law is void and may be revoked and seized by the chief of police or his designee, subject to appeal rights as provided in subsection 12-162(e) of this article.
(g) All permits issued hereunder remain the property of the police department and shall be produced for inspection upon the demand of any officer or designee of the city police department.
(h) Replacement permits will be issued within 30 days of the original date, upon paying one-half of the fee charged for pawnshop permits. After 30 days of the original application date, a new application and fee must be submitted.
(Ord. No. 2013-12, § 1(Exh. A), 12-10-2013)

345:
(a) Contents of daily report.
(1) Every pawnshop shall prepare a daily report in writing for the police department, in such form as may be prescribed by the police department or any designated agent, of all property that has been the subject of a pawn transaction during the 24 hours ending at 8:00 p.m. on the date of the report. Such report shall be computer generated, typewritten or printed legibly. In addition to any other information required by the police department, the report shall show:
a. The name and address of the licensee;
b. Time of transaction;
c. Serial number of pawn tickets;
d. Amount paid or advanced;
e. Full description of articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stones in jewelry, and all identifying names, marks and numbers; and
f. A description of persons pledging, selling or pawning, including name, address, driver's license number, weight and height.
(2) Serial numbers on items with serial numbers must be included in this report. Insufficient reports shall be rejected, and any licensee or representative of licensee making such report shall be deemed guilty of an offense punishable according to the provisions of section 1-12. The daily report shall be in such form and contain such information as may be required by the police chief or by his designated agent, and sufficient information to identify each of such articles. Any person subject to this article, any licensee or employee of a licensee, who shall fail or refuse to prepare and keep reports as provided in this section or shall make false entries concerning the transactions named, or who shall fail or refuse to permit an inspection or examination by any police officer of the reports and of the property pledged or sold to licensee shall be punished as provided for in section 1-12.
(b) The report shall be signed and dated by the licensee, if an individual, or by the responsible person, if a corporate or other business entity. The licensee, as well as the person signing the daily report, is responsible for ensuring the completeness and accuracy of the report.
(c) The failure of any licensee or representative of the licensee to comply with the provisions of this section shall constitute an offense punishable as provided in section 1-12.
(Ord. No. 2013-12, § 1(Exh. A), 12-10-2013)

352:
(a) Proceeds of the tax. The proceeds of the tax shall be used in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-13-51(a)(3).
(b) Expenditure of proceeds.
(1) A portion of the taxes collected shall be paid to the county chamber of commerce or any other entity or organization allowable under O.C.G.A. § 48-13-41 and as may be amended in the future pursuant to a contract with same, and expended for the purposes of promoting tourism, conventions, and trade shows in the city. In this regard, the county chamber of commerce must spend such monies on planning, conducting or participating in programs of information and publicity designed to attract or advertise tourism, conventions or trade shows in the city. The portions to be paid to the county chamber of commerce shall be as follows: An amount equal to the amount by which the total taxes collected under this article exceed the taxes which would have been collected at a rate of three percent;
(2) Prior to the beginning of each fiscal year, the county chamber of commerce shall provide to the governing authority a budget detailing the proposed expenditure of the funds provided under this article. Such budget, after being approved by the governing authority, shall be made a part of the budget for the city;
(3) The county chamber of commerce shall provide to the governing authority audit verification that the county chamber of commerce has made use of the funds in conformity with the proposed budget and the general purpose of this article. The county chamber of commerce shall also complete a department of community affairs hotel/motel tax report and project/contractor information schedule.
(Ord. of 2-4-2008, § 18-114.5)

375:
(a) Any nonprofit person, firm, association, corporation or organization desiring a permit to solicit or canvass upon public areas or to call from house to house in the city shall file, on a form to be supplied by the chief of police, an application stating the following:
(1) Name of applicant;
(2) Permanent residence address;
(3) Name, address and telephone number of employer, firm, corporation or organization represented;
(4) Names and address of all persons assisting in the solicitation, canvassing or calling from house to house in the city; and
(5) Nature of any merchandise, wares, goods or any similar items to be sold or offered for sale or given away in conjunction with the solicitation, canvassing or calling from house to house in the city.
(b) Proof of tax-exempt status shall be required when registering with the chief of police.

380:
It shall be unlawful for a solicitor, canvasser or caller to do any of the following:
(1) To falsely represent, directly or by implication, that funds being solicited are on behalf of any person, firm, corporation, association or organization other than such registered with the chief of police;
(2) To solicit, canvass or call without the express prior permission of any occupant or property owner at any residence, apartment complex, or shopping mall where there is posted a sign forbidding such activity;
(3) To remain on private premises after being asked to leave the premises; or
(4) To continue solicitation after being refused upon public areas; such action shall constitute harassment.

386:
Applicants for permits under this article must file with the chief of police a sworn application in writing on a form to be furnished by the city, which shall give the following information:
(1) Name and description of the applicant;
(2) Address;
(3) A brief description of the nature of the business and the goods to be sold;
(4) If employed, the name and address of the employer;
(5) The length of time for which the right to do business is desired;
(6) If a vehicle is to be used, a description of the same, together with license number or other means of identification;
(7) If a fixed location is o be used, the address and specific location within such address; and
(8) A statement as to whether or not the applicant has been convicted of any crime, misdemeanor, or violation of any municipal ordinance involving theft, deception or injury to persons, the nature of the offense and the punishment or penalty assessed therefor.

392:
Permits issued under the provisions of this article may be revoked for any of the following causes:
(1) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statement contained in the application for permit;
(2) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statement made in the course of carrying on any business contemplated hereunder;
(3) Any violation of this article;
(4) Conviction of any crime or misdemeanor involving moral turpitude; or
(5) Conducting any business contemplated hereunder, in an unlawful manner or in such a manner as to constitute a breach of the peace or to constitute a menace to the health, safety, or general welfare of the public.
